Short answer: yes, with an important nuance.

What AGENTS.md actually says
The "Autonomous Beads workflow" is explicitly one-issue-per-session. The lifecycle diagram reads hermes-run (one Hermes session = one issue), and the rules say "Do exactly ONE issue per session." So the file does encode "do one, then stop."

But it's not "just" the file driving me
- Your own request said "Do one issue, then stop." So the stopping is a deliberate convergence of two sources — AGENTS.md and your instruction — not me blindly obeying a doc.
- "One issue" means one ticket (a full unit of work: claim → tests-first → code → quality gate → PR), not "halt after the first sub-step." I don't stop the moment I finish any single action.
- AGENTS.md is project context I load and follow, but my system prompt and your instructions also bind me, and explicit user/orchestrator direction overrides the file. So if you say "keep going," I do.

Why one issue per session (the design intent)
It's how the unattended harness works: hermes-run starts a fresh session with no memory of the previous one, so each run does exactly one ticket and a reviewer (hermes-review) decides the next. Dependents stay blocked until a PR merges, which bounds blast radius and gives a human a review point between tickets. The stop markers NO_READY_WORK / RUN_BLOCKED are printed specifically so that harness can classify the run.

One caveat worth flagging
AGENTS.md scopes that full "commit/push/open-PR" opt-in to unattended hermes-run sessions and says "Interactive sessions keep the Conservative profile." You invoked this workflow explicitly, so I'm following it — but it's an opt-in you granted, not a default I'd assume in a plain interactive chat.

Where I am right now
I'm mid-task on organize_music-6ut.10 (the Phase 0 end-of-phase review) — not finished. So far I've: claimed it, branched from origin/main, run the gate (210 tests pass, ruff clean), confirmed all 9 Phase 0 issues are closed, and found that 3 of 4 "pure" modules hit 100% coverage after I added the missing-branch tests — while the remaining gap in naming.py is exactly the empty-stem bug that the open P2 issue organize_music-f67 already documents, so I did not lock that in.

